            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm trying to understand why we need a string in the option parameter\u2014couldn't we just use an enum type instead?\n\nAlso, why do we need a boolean for the provided option? If the caller provides the option, we could simply rely on it to determine behavior, such as forcing a failover.\n\nLastly, I'm wondering why a dictionary is needed. It seems like the plan is to have a single argument for this method and extend it in the future by adding new option enums and passing them along, is it correct?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I was basing it on https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/blob/master/yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Dump/Create.interface.yaml#L17.\n\nSome options could have values, such as maybe Target = <some object path>, so that's why it wouldn't just be a list of enums."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "OK"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "typo? Options?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 19,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "I think we need a clear error message explaining why the failover was rejected so that the caller can decide whether to retry later or implement monitoring to determine when the failover can be triggered again. This could be revisited if a specific use case arises."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Control/Failover.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 19,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Was debating that.  Right now I've come up with 7 reasons, with possibly more coming.  Some could apply pretty generically to all failovers, like 'redundancy isn't enabled', but some are more specific to BMC ones like 'active BMC isn't responding but last time it was redundancy wasn't enabled.'"
                }
            ],
